<BaseLayout title="Power Steering" description="Hydraulic power steering adapters, pressure reducer, and electric power steering for LS Genesis Coupe swaps.">
  <h1>Power Steering</h1>
  <div class="note"><p><strong>Use Pentosin CHF 202 synthetic hydraulic fluid.</strong></p></div>

  <h2>Hydraulic (GM Pump)</h2>
  <h3>Option A: Sikky Line Kit</h3>
  <p>Sikky sells a Genesis power steering line kit (PS105, $167.99) for LS1/LS2-style pumps, either alone or as an add-on to their swap kit. It's the bolt-in route.</p>

  <h3>Option B: Adapt the Factory Lines</h3>
  <p>
    If you're not getting the Sikky line kit, you'll need an adapter for the high-pressure PS line that ends near the front subframe.
    Don't cheap out: other adapters have been tried and leak. Or mount directly to the rack with the Russell fitting below.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="https://www.summitracing.com/parts/krs-krc10122000" target="_blank" rel="noopener">KRC 10122000</a>: steel, male -6 AN to male M16 x 1.5 O-ring, straight (O-rings included).
      Summit currently lists it as unavailable, so check other KRC dealers.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="https://www.summitracing.com/parts/rus-648060" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Russell 648060</a>: aluminum, -6 AN to 16mm x 1.5 O-ring, straight. Adapts directly to the rack.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Pressure Reducer</h3>
  <p>
    The GM pump puts out about <strong>1200 psi</strong>; the Genesis rack needs about <strong>850 psi</strong>. Use a pressure reducer kit with 2 or 3 of the supplied shims.
    Some have gotten away without shims, so this is debatable.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Example: <a href="https://www.speedwaymotors.com/Tuff-Stuff-5552-Power-Steering-Pressure-Valve-850-PSI,278221.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tuff Stuff 5552 850 psi pressure valve</a> (about $23), used by LS swappers to reduce pressure on GM Type II pumps.
      Confirm it fits your specific pump before ordering.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <AccessoryDrive />

  <h2>Electric Power Steering</h2>
  <p>By Alex Toothman on the Genesis Coupe LS Swaps Facebook page.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Video: <a href="https://www.facebook.com/share/v/185cA96S7q/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Facebook</a></li>
    <li>CAN bus controller, -6AN adapter block, and empty connectors: <a href="https://reformmotorsports.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Reform Motorsports</a></li>
    <li>Reservoir if you buy a Jeep/Durango pump: <a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0FPCGJWJD" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Amazon</a></li>
  </ul>
